    "tea": "Acts eighteen. A man named Apollos shows up in Ephesus. Born in Alexandria. The text calls him eloquent, and mighty in the scriptures. He teaches diligently the things of the Lord. He is genuinely gifted. And then one clause changes everything. \"Knowing only the baptism of John.\" He is brilliant, and he is behind. He has most of it, and he is preaching with total confidence. So he starts speaking boldly in the synagogue. And Aquila and Priscilla hear him. And here is the move. They do not stand up in the middle of the synagogue and correct him publicly. They do not go home and subtweet him. \"Whom when Aquila and Priscilla had heard, they took him unto them, and expounded unto him the way of God more perfectly.\" Took him aside. Filled in what was missing. Protected the ministry while they fixed it. And Apollos goes on to help the believers enormously and to convince people publicly. One more thing, and I want to be straight with you. In several places her name is listed first. Including this one. Luke never tells us why, so treat the theories as theories. What you can see on the page is that she taught the teacher.",
    "ref": "Acts 18:26",
    "book": "Acts",
    "chapter": 18,
