    "tea": "Joppa. Her name is Tabitha. In Greek, Dorcas. Luke describes her in one line. \"Full of good works and almsdeeds which she did.\" Not that she was kind. What she did. Then she dies. They wash her. Carry her upstairs. The disciples hear Peter is one town over and send two men, begging. Come. Do not wait. He comes. They take him upstairs. And this is the part. \"All the widows stood by him weeping, and shewing the coats and garments which Dorcas made, while she was with them.\" They are holding up the clothes. That is the eulogy. Not words. Evidence. Understand what a widow was then. No income. No safety net. This woman had quietly been dressing them. Now watch Peter. \"But Peter put them all forth, and kneeled down, and prayed.\" Put them all forth. Years earlier, Peter was one of three men Jesus let into a room with a dead girl. Mark says Jesus \"put them all out.\" Knelt. And said, \"Talitha cumi.\" Peter clears the room the same way. And says, \"Tabitha, arise.\" One letter apart. He was in the room the day he learned how to do that.",
